    "narrative": "Our projected offensive output for May 5th centers on three primary cornerstones, with **James Wood** leading the way at a 70.96 efficiency. **James Wood** is our standout play today; hitting leadoff against a manageable 4.27 xERA arm while boasting a robust 0.377 xwOBA makes him an elite engine for counting stats. Joining him in the elite tier are **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.58) and **Corbin Carroll** (70.10). **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits significantly from a high-value lineup spot and a favorable platoon advantage against a 4.06 xERA starter, while **Corbin Carroll** remains a perennial start due to his dual-threat capacity, despite a middling matchup. **Seiya Suzuki** also warrants attention; while his raw base is lower, his high efficiency (68.46) is bolstered by favorable park conditions and an aggressive stolen base environment against a slower-to-the-plate pitcher.\n\nThe benching of **Alex Freeland** was our most difficult decision, as he narrowly missed the floor with a 40.41 score. Ultimately, the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Cole Young** despite the latter\u2019s tough park context, as **Alex Freeland** was hampered by an unfavorable batting order spot. We are also exercising caution with **Austin Riley** (47.22) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (44.14); both are startable but face significant headwinds today\u2014**Austin Riley** is negated by a tough matchup against an elite 3.53 xERA arm, and **Tyler Soderstrom** carries the highest risk of the group, with an SP skill penalty dropping his viability significantly against a 3.02 xERA southpaw.\n\nRegarding roster risks, we have already cleared **Mickey Moniak** from active consideration due to a confirmed rainout, and we are monitoring **Jose Caballero** closely. Despite his elite potential for stolen bases, **Jose Caballero** remains benched today (39.32) because he draws an extremely difficult matchup against a top-tier arm, failing to clear our 40.0 efficiency floor. We are proceeding with **Trevor Story** in the SS slot (59.08); while he isn't in the elite tier, the slow delivery of the opposing pitcher creates a prime green light for his speed, adding a layer of hidden value to his projected stat line that pushes him well above the marginal threshold.",
